   * each is the same rule it was before, expressed in SQL:
   *
   *   business only     `bus_` prefix or `entity_type = 'U'`. The dictionary's
   *                     own `sys_*` tables are the Application Dictionary, not
   *                     the application, and they have their own section.
   *   not a line item   `%%entity <Child> parent: <Parent>` gives the child no
   *                     window and puts its tab inside the parent's at
   *                     `tab_level` 1. A line item is reached by opening a
   *                     parent record, so it is not a place to navigate to —
   *                     the NOT EXISTS below is that rule, read off `sys_tab`
   *                     rather than recomputed so this screen and the detail
   *                     screen cannot disagree.
   *   readable          `%%rbac … .read`. A table no read directive names is
   *                     unrestricted and stays visible; a table some directive
   *                     restricts is visible only to a role that holds it.
   *                     `EntityAccessGuard` enforces the same rule on the way
   *                     in — this applies it one screen earlier, because a
   *                     dashboard that offers nine entities and then answers
   *                     403 on them cannot be told from a broken one.
   *   active            `is_active` on both sides.
   *
   * A FULL OUTER JOIN over a filtered CTE rather than a plain join, because
   * both unmatched sides are wanted: a category with no visible entity (which
   * a master still sees, as an empty section it can fill) and an entity with no
   * category (the `Uncategorized` bucket).
   *
   * Role names are folded the same way `EntityAccessGuard` folds them — case
   * and separators both, because `sys_operation_access` holds the model's
   * spelling (`computational_chemist`) and `sys_role` the displayed one
   * (`Computational Chemist`). Folding case alone makes every multi-word role
   * unequal to itself.
   *
   * Nothing the caller sends reaches this SQL. `isMaster` is a boolean and the
   * role list is bound as a parameter array.
   */
  /**
   * The whole dashboard, in one statement.
   *
   * Everything the screen draws: the categories with the entities inside them,
   * the admin windows this caller may open, and who the caller is as far as the
   * dictionary is concerned. One round trip, and the roles are resolved inside
   * it rather than handed in.
   *
   * It replaces two HTTP calls and six queries. `/sys/categories/with-entities`
   * was four (`sys_category`, `sys_table`, `sys_operation_access`, and
   * `sys_tab ⋈ sys_table`), `/me/permissions` was two more (the caller's roles,
   * then the windows granted to them) — and the second pair could not be folded
   * into the first while the roles arrived from the session object instead of
   * from the database.
   *
   * Resolving the roles in SQL is what makes one statement possible, and it is
   * also the more correct of the two. `sys_operation_access` holds the model's
   * spelling of a role (`billing_clerk`) and `sys_role` the displayed one
   * (`Billing Clerk`); folding both sides here means the entity scope and the
   * window grants are decided against the same rows, rather than against a
   * session object that may carry either spelling or neither.
   *
   * Three JSON aggregates rather than a UNION of differently-shaped rows: the
   * categories and the windows have nothing in common but the request they
   * answer, and forcing them into one column list would mean a dozen nullable
   * columns and a discriminator to unpick at the other end.
   *
   * Nothing the caller sends is interpolated. The user identity is bound, and
   * every predicate is built from dictionary rows.
   */
  async dashboard(user?: { id?: string; sysUserId?: string; email?: string; isMaster?: boolean }) {
    const sysUserId = typeof user?.sysUserId === 'string' ? user.sysUserId : null;
    const email = typeof user?.email === 'string' ? user.email : null;

    const result = await sql<{
      categories: unknown;
      windows: unknown;
      is_master: boolean;
      unscoped: boolean;
      role_names: string[] | null;
    }>`
      WITH me AS (
        SELECT su.sys_user_id
          FROM sys_user su
         WHERE (${sysUserId}::uuid IS NOT NULL AND su.sys_user_id = ${sysUserId}::uuid)
            OR (${sysUserId}::uuid IS NULL AND ${email}::text IS NOT NULL AND su.email = ${email}::text)
         LIMIT 1
      ),
      myroles AS (
        SELECT r.sys_role_id, r.name, r.is_master_role
          FROM sys_user_roles ur
          JOIN sys_role r ON r.sys_role_id = ur.sys_role_id
         WHERE ur.sys_user_id = (SELECT sys_user_id FROM me)
           AND ur.is_active
           AND r.is_active
      ),
      /*
       * Two questions that are not the same question, answered separately
       * because conflating them hid every entity from the wrong people.
       *
       *   unscoped   may this caller see every entity? A master role may, and
       *              so does a caller with no dictionary identity at all —
       *              the same fail-open the access guard takes on a database
       *              that predates the rbac migration.
       *   is_master  is this caller actually an administrator? Only the master
       *              role is. A user the dictionary has never heard of is not
       *              an administrator, they are unmapped, and the dashboard
       *              must not treat the two alike.
       */
      scope AS (
        SELECT COALESCE((SELECT bool_or(is_master_role) FROM myroles), false) AS is_master,
               (NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM me))
               OR COALESCE((SELECT bool_or(is_master_role) FROM myroles), false) AS unscoped
      ),
      /* The model's spelling and the displayed one, folded the same way
         EntityAccessGuard folds them: case and separators both. */
      held AS (
        SELECT lower(regexp_replace(name, '[[:space:]_-]+', '', 'g')) AS folded FROM myroles
      ),
      visible AS (
        SELECT t.sys_table_id, t.table_name, t.name, t.description, t.icon,
               t.entity_type, t.sys_category_id
          FROM sys_table t
         WHERE t.is_active
           AND (t.table_name LIKE 'bus\_%' ESCAPE '\' OR t.entity_type = 'U')
           AND NOT EXISTS (
                 SELECT 1 FROM sys_tab tb
                  WHERE tb.sys_table_id = t.sys_table_id AND tb.tab_level > 0)
           AND (
                 (SELECT unscoped FROM scope)
                 OR NOT EXISTS (
                      SELECT 1 FROM sys_operation_access oa
                       WHERE oa.table_name = t.table_name
                         AND oa.operation = 'read' AND oa.is_active)
                 OR EXISTS (
                      SELECT 1 FROM sys_operation_access oa
                       WHERE oa.table_name = t.table_name
                         AND oa.operation = 'read' AND oa.is_active
                         AND lower(regexp_replace(oa.role_name, '[[:space:]_-]+', '', 'g'))
                             IN (SELECT folded FROM held))
               )
      ),
      grouped AS (
        SELECT c.sys_category_id, c.name AS category_name, c.code,
               c.description AS category_description, c.icon AS category_icon,
               c.color, c.seq_no,
               v.sys_table_id, v.table_name, v.name AS entity_name,
               v.description AS entity_description, v.icon AS entity_icon,
               v.entity_type
          FROM sys_category c
          FULL OUTER JOIN visible v ON v.sys_category_id = c.sys_category_id
         WHERE c.sys_category_id IS NULL OR c.is_active
      ),
      win AS (
        SELECT DISTINCT w.sys_window_id, w.name, w.description AS route, w.icon,
               w.entity_type, bool_and(a.is_read_only) AS is_read_only
          FROM sys_access a
          JOIN sys_window w ON w.sys_window_id = a.sys_window_id
         WHERE a.sys_role_id IN (SELECT sys_role_id FROM myroles)
           AND a.is_exclude = false
           AND w.is_active
         GROUP BY w.sys_window_id, w.name, w.description, w.icon, w.entity_type
      )
      SELECT
        (SELECT json_agg(row_to_json(g) ORDER BY g.category_name NULLS LAST, g.entity_name)
           FROM grouped g)                                            AS categories,
        (SELECT json_agg(row_to_json(x) ORDER BY x.name) FROM win x)  AS windows,
        (SELECT is_master FROM scope)                                 AS is_master,
        (SELECT unscoped FROM scope)                                  AS unscoped,
        (SELECT array_agg(name ORDER BY name) FROM myroles)           AS role_names
    `.execute(this.db.kysely);

    const row = result.rows[0];
    const rows = (row?.categories as DashboardEntityRow[] | null) ?? [];
    const windows = (row?.windows as DashboardWindowRow[] | null) ?? [];

    return {
      data: this.groupDashboardRows(rows, Boolean(row?.unscoped)),
      windows: windows.map((w) => ({
        sys_window_id: w.sys_window_id,
        name: w.name,
        route: w.route,
        icon: w.icon ?? null,
        category: w.entity_type === 'S' ? 'admin' : 'business',
        is_read_only: w.is_read_only,
      })),
      role: row?.role_names?.[0] ?? 'user',
      isMaster: Boolean(row?.is_master),
    };
  }

  private async dashboardRows(user?: {
    isMaster?: boolean;
    roles?: string[];
    sysRoles?: string[];
    role?: string;
  }) {
    const foldRole = (role: string) => role.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/[\s\-_]+/g, '');
    const held: string[] = [];
    for (const value of [...(user?.sysRoles ?? []), ...(user?.roles ?? []), user?.role]) {
      if (typeof value === 'string' && value) held.push(foldRole(value));
    }

    // No user at all means no scoping decision to make — the same fail-open the
    // guard takes on a database that predates the rbac migration.
    const unscoped = !user || user.isMaster === true;

    const rows = await sql<{
      sys_category_id: string | null;
      category_name: string | null;
      code: string | null;
      category_description: string | null;
      category_icon: string | null;
      color: string | null;
      seq_no: number | null;
      sys_table_id: string | null;
      table_name: string | null;
      entity_name: string | null;
      entity_description: string | null;
      entity_icon: string | null;
      entity_type: string | null;
    }>`
      WITH visible AS (
        SELECT t.sys_table_id,
               t.table_name,
               t.name        AS entity_name,
               t.description AS entity_description,
               t.icon        AS entity_icon,
               t.entity_type,
               t.sys_category_id
          FROM sys_table t
         WHERE t.is_active
           AND (t.table_name LIKE 'bus\_%' ESCAPE '\' OR t.entity_type = 'U')
           AND NOT EXISTS (
                 SELECT 1 FROM sys_tab tb
                  WHERE tb.sys_table_id = t.sys_table_id
                    AND tb.tab_level > 0)
           AND (
                 ${unscoped}
                 OR NOT EXISTS (
                      SELECT 1 FROM sys_operation_access oa
                       WHERE oa.table_name = t.table_name
                         AND oa.operation = 'read'
                         AND oa.is_active)
                 OR EXISTS (
                      SELECT 1 FROM sys_operation_access oa
                       WHERE oa.table_name = t.table_name
                         AND oa.operation = 'read'
                         AND oa.is_active
                         AND lower(regexp_replace(oa.role_name, '[[:space:]_-]+', '', 'g'))
                             = ANY(${held}))
               )
      )
      SELECT c.sys_category_id,
             c.name        AS category_name,
             c.code,
             c.description AS category_description,
             c.icon        AS category_icon,
             c.color,
             c.seq_no,
             v.sys_table_id,
             v.table_name,
             v.entity_name,
             v.entity_description,
             v.entity_icon,
             v.entity_type
        FROM sys_category c
        FULL OUTER JOIN visible v ON v.sys_category_id = c.sys_category_id
       WHERE c.sys_category_id IS NULL OR c.is_active
       ORDER BY c.name NULLS LAST, v.entity_name
    `.execute(this.db.kysely);

    return { rows: rows.rows, unscoped };
  }

  /**
   * Bucket the dashboard's entity rows into category groups.
   *
   * Shared by `dashboard()` and `findAllWithEntities()` so the two cannot
   * disagree about what a group is: same ordering, same Uncategorized bucket,
   * same rule for dropping a group a scoped reader has no entity in.
   */
  private groupDashboardRows(rows: DashboardEntityRow[], unscoped: boolean) {

    const groups = new Map<string, any>();
    const orphans: any[] = [];

    for (const row of rows) {
      const entity = row.sys_table_id
        ? {
            sys_table_id: row.sys_table_id,
            table_name: row.table_name,
            name: row.entity_name,
            description: row.entity_description,
            icon: row.entity_icon,
            entity_type: row.entity_type,
            sys_category_id: row.sys_category_id,
            is_active: true,
          }
        : null;

      // An entity with no category: the FULL JOIN's unmatched right side.
      if (!row.sys_category_id) {
        if (entity) orphans.push(entity);
        continue;
      }

      const group =
        groups.get(row.sys_category_id) ??
        {
          sys_category_id: row.sys_category_id,
          name: row.category_name,
          code: row.code,
          description: row.category_description,
          icon: row.category_icon,
          color: row.color,
          seq_no: row.seq_no ?? 0,
          is_default: false,
          is_active: true,
          entities: [] as any[],
        };
      if (entity) group.entities.push(entity);
      groups.set(row.sys_category_id, group);
    }

    // A group whose every entity belongs to another role is not an empty group,
    // it is somebody else's. A card reading "0 entities" tells a scoped reader
    // nothing they can act on — but an unscoped one is looking at the dictionary
    // itself, where an empty category is a category waiting to be filled.
    const result = [...groups.values()].filter((g) => unscoped || g.entities.length > 0);

    if (orphans.length > 0) {
      result.push({
        sys_category_id: null,
        name: 'Uncategorized',
        code: 'uncategorized',
        description: 'Entities not yet assigned to a category',
        icon: 'circle-help',
        color: null,
        seq_no: 9999,
        is_default: false,
        is_active: true,
        entities: orphans,
      });
    }

    // Alphabetical by name, with Uncategorized last regardless of spelling.
    result.sort((a: any, b: any) => {
      if (a.sys_category_id === null) return 1;
      if (b.sys_category_id === null) return -1;
      return String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name));
    });

    return result;
  }

  async findAllWithEntities(user?: { isMaster?: boolean; roles?: string[]; sysRoles?: string[]; role?: string }) {
    const { rows, unscoped } = await this.dashboardRows(user);
    const result = this.groupDashboardRows(rows as DashboardEntityRow[], unscoped);
    return { data: result, meta: { total: result.length } };
  }

  async create(dto: CreateCategoryDto) {
    if (!dto.name?.trim()) throw new BadRequestException('name is required');

    const code = (dto.code?.trim() || slugify(dto.name));
    if (!code) throw new BadRequestException('code could not be derived from name');

    const clash = await this.db.kysely
      .selectFrom('sys_category')
      .select('sys_category_id')
      .where((eb) => eb.or([eb('name', '=', dto.name.trim()), eb('code', '=', code)]))
      .executeTakeFirst();

    if (clash) throw new ConflictException(`A category with that name or code already exists`);

    if (dto.is_default) await this.clearDefault();

    const id = uuidv4();
    await this.db.kysely
      .insertInto('sys_category')
      .values({
        sys_category_id: id,
        name: dto.name.trim(),
        code,
        description: dto.description ?? null,
        icon: dto.icon ?? null,
        color: dto.color ?? null,
        seq_no: dto.seq_no ?? 0,
        is_default: dto.is_default ?? false,
        is_active: dto.is_active ?? true,
        created_at: new Date(),
        updated_at: new Date(),
      } as any)
      .execute();

    return this.findById(id);
  }

  async update(id: string, dto: UpdateCategoryDto) {
    await this.findById(id);

    if (dto.name !== undefined && !dto.name.trim()) {
      throw new BadRequestException('name cannot be empty');
    }

    if (dto.name || dto.code) {
      const clash = await this.db.kysely
        .selectFrom('sys_category')
        .select('sys_category_id')
        .where('sys_category_id', '!=', id)
        .where((eb) => {
          const conditions = [] as any[];
          if (dto.name) conditions.push(eb('name', '=', dto.name.trim()));
          if (dto.code) conditions.push(eb('code', '=', dto.code.trim()));
          return eb.or(conditions);
        })
        .executeTakeFirst();

      if (clash) throw new ConflictException('A category with that name or code already exists');
    }

    if (dto.is_default) await this.clearDefault(id);

    const patch: Record<string, unknown> = { updated_at: new Date() };
    for (const key of ['name', 'code', 'description', 'icon', 'color', 'seq_no', 'is_default', 'is_active'] as const) {
      if (dto[key] !== undefined) patch[key] = typeof dto[key] === 'string' ? (dto[key] as string).trim() : dto[key];
    }

    await this.db.kysely
      .updateTable('sys_category')
      .set(patch as any)
      .where('sys_category_id', '=', id)
      .execute();

    return this.findById(id);
  }

  /**
   * Deactivate a category. Entities pointing at it are detached rather than
   * deleted — losing an entity because its grouping was removed would be a
   * surprising amount of collateral damage.
   */
  async remove(id: string) {
    await this.findById(id);

    await this.db.kysely
      .updateTable('sys_table')
      .set({ sys_category_id: null } as any)
      .where('sys_category_id', '=', id)
      .execute();

    await this.db.kysely
      .updateTable('sys_category')
      .set({ is_active: false, updated_at: new Date() } as any)
      .where('sys_category_id', '=', id)
      .execute();

    return { message: 'Category deactivated and its entities detached' };
  }

  /** Assign a set of entities to a category (or to none, when categoryId is null). */
  async assignEntities(categoryId: string | null, tableIds: string[]) {
    if (categoryId) await this.findById(categoryId);
    if (tableIds.length === 0) return { updated: 0 };

    await this.db.kysely
      .updateTable('sys_table')
      .set({ sys_category_id: categoryId } as any)
      .where('sys_table_id', 'in', tableIds)
      .execute();

    return { updated: tableIds.length };
  }

  /** Entities belonging to one category. */
  async entitiesFor(id: string) {
    await this.findById(id);
    return this.db.kysely
      .selectFrom('sys_table')
      .select(['sys_table_id', 'table_name', 'name', 'description', 'entity_type'])
      .where('sys_category_id', '=', id)
      .where('is_active', '=', true)
      .orderBy('name', 'asc')
      .execute();
  }

  private async clearDefault(exceptId?: string) {
    let query = this.db.kysely.updateTable('sys_category').set({ is_default: false } as any);
    if (exceptId) query = query.where('sys_category_id', '!=', exceptId);
    await query.execute();
  }
}

/**
 * System Configuration Service
 *
 * Reads configuration from sys_system with a fallback chain:
 *   DB row (active + non-empty) -> process.env -> hardcoded default.
 *
 * The in-memory cache is loaded on startup and refreshed on admin writes,
 * so a read never hits the database during normal request handling.
 *
 * Generated: {{now}}
 */

import { Injectable, OnModuleInit, Inject } from '@nestjs/common';
import { Kysely } from 'kysely';
import { KYSELY_CONNECTION } from '../../../database/database.constants';

interface CachedConfig {
  value: string;
  dataType: string;
  isActive: boolean;
}

@Injectable()
export class SystemConfigService implements OnModuleInit {
  private cache = new Map<string, CachedConfig>();

  // KYSELY_CONNECTION is the token DatabaseModule actually provides; nothing in
  // the tree has ever provided 'DATABASE_CONNECTION', so injecting that name
  // failed Nest's dependency resolution at boot and the application did not
  // start at all.
  constructor(@Inject(KYSELY_CONNECTION) private readonly db: Kysely<any>) {}

  async onModuleInit(): Promise<void> {
    await this.refresh();
  }

  async refresh(): Promise<void> {
    const rows = await this.db
      .selectFrom('sys_system')
      .select(['config_key', 'config_value', 'data_type', 'is_active'])
      .execute();

    this.cache.clear();
    for (const row of rows) {
      this.cache.set(row.config_key as string, {
        value: (row.config_value ?? '') as string,
        dataType: (row.data_type ?? 'string') as string,
        isActive: row.is_active as boolean,
      });
    }
  }

  get(key: string, fallback?: string): string {
    const cached = this.cache.get(key);
    if (cached?.isActive && cached.value !== '') {
      return cached.value;
    }
    return process.env[key] ?? fallback ?? '';
  }

  getNumber(key: string, fallback: number): number {
    const raw = this.get(key, String(fallback));
    const parsed = Number(raw);
    return Number.isNaN(parsed) ? fallback : parsed;
  }

  getBoolean(key: string, fallback: boolean): boolean {
    const raw = this.get(key, String(fallback));
    return raw === 'true' || raw === '1';
  }

  getAppDescription(): string {
    return this.get('APP_DESCRIPTION', 'A business application generated by APPWITHAI.');
  }

  getAppName(): string {
    return this.get('APP_NAME', '{{project.name}}');
  }

  async list(): Promise<any[]> {
    return this.db
      .selectFrom('sys_system')
      .selectAll()
      .orderBy('category')
      .orderBy('config_key')
      .execute();
  }

  async update(
    id: string,
    data: { config_value?: string; is_active?: boolean; description?: string },
    userId?: string
  ): Promise<any> {
    const result = await this.db
      .updateTable('sys_system')
      .set({
        ...data,
        updated_by: userId,
        updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),
      })
      .where('sys_system_id', '=', id)
      .returningAll()
      .executeTakeFirst();

    await this.refresh();
    return result;
  }

  async create(
    data: {
      config_key: string;
      config_value?: string;
      data_type?: string;
      category?: string;
      description?: string;
      is_sensitive?: boolean;
    },
    userId?: string
  ): Promise<any> {
    const result = await this.db
      .insertInto('sys_system')
      .values({
        ...data,
        is_active: true,
        created_by: userId,
        updated_by: userId,
        created_at: new Date().toISOString(),
        updated_at: new Date().toISOString(),
      })
      .returningAll()
      .executeTakeFirst();

    await this.refresh();
    return result;
  }

  async remove(id: string): Promise<void> {
    await this.db
      .deleteFrom('sys_system')
      .where('sys_system_id', '=', id)
      .execute();

    await this.refresh();
  }
}

/**
 * SysReport Service
 *
 * The questions the model declared with `%%report`, and the queries that answer
 * them. Each row in `sys_report` carries SQL authored in the `.eml.mmd`
 * document; running one is the only place in this application where stored text
 * becomes a statement, so most of what follows is about that.
 *
 * Not to be confused with the report *designs* service, which renders one
 * record as a printable document. This is analysis over the whole database.
 *
 * Generated: {{now}}
 * Project: {{project.name}}
 */

import { BadRequestException, Injectable, NotFoundException } from '@nestjs/common';
import { sql } from 'kysely';
import { DatabaseService } from '../../../database/database.service';

export interface SysReportRecord {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  title: string;
  entity_name: string | null;
  table_name: string | null;
  chart: 'bar' | 'line' | 'pie' | 'area' | null;
  x_axis: string | null;
  y_axis: string | null;
  help: string | null;
  sql_text: string;
  sort_order: number;
}

export interface ReportResult {
  report: Omit<SysReportRecord, 'sql_text'>;
  columns: string[];
  rows: Record<string, unknown>[];
  rowCount: number;
  truncated: boolean;
  durationMs: number;
}

/**
 * The most rows a report will return.
 *
 * A report is a question a person reads, not an export. A model is free to
 * declare a query with no LIMIT — most of them do, because the interesting ones
 * are already narrowed by their WHERE clause — and on a seeded database that is
 * fine. On a real one, "every row of bus_transaction" is a query that ends the
 * tab rather than answering anything. The cap is applied as an outer LIMIT so
 * the report's own ordering still decides *which* rows, and the response says
 * when it bit.
 */
const MAX_ROWS = 5000;

/**
 * A report may only read, checked again here.
 *
 * The generator refuses a non-SELECT at compile time, so a statement that is
 * not a query cannot reach a seed file. This is the second check, and it is not
 * redundant: `sys_report` is an ordinary table, and anything that can write to
 * it — a later migration, a restored backup, an administrator with database
 * access — could otherwise put a statement here that this service would run
 * with the application's own credentials.
 */
function assertReadOnly(text: string): void {
  const body = text.replace(/;\s*$/, '');
  if (!/^\s*(?:with|select)\b/i.test(body)) {
    throw new BadRequestException('A report query must be a SELECT or a WITH query.');
  }
  // A semicolon outside quotes means a second statement is hiding behind the
  // first: `SELECT 1; DROP TABLE bus_account` passes the test above.
  let quote: string | null = null;
  for (let i = 0; i < body.length; i++) {
    const ch = body[i];
    if (quote) {
      if (ch === quote) {
        if (body[i + 1] === quote) i += 1;
        else quote = null;
      }
      continue;
    }
    if (ch === "'" || ch === '"') quote = ch;
    else if (ch === ';') {
      throw new BadRequestException('A report query must be a single statement.');
    }
  }
}

@Injectable()
export class SysReportService {
  constructor(private readonly db: DatabaseService) {}

  /**
   * Every report, grouped in the order the screen shows them.
   *
   * `sql_text` is deliberately not returned. The list feeds a menu; handing the
   * query to the browser would put the application's schema in front of anyone
   * who can open the reports page, and nothing on that screen needs it.
   */
  async findAll(): Promise<Omit<SysReportRecord, 'sql_text'>[]> {
    const rows = await this.db.kysely
      .selectFrom('sys_report' as never)
      .select([
        'id',
        'name',
        'title',
        'entity_name',
        'table_name',
        'chart',
        'x_axis',
        'y_axis',
        'help',
        'sort_order',
      ] as never)
      .orderBy('sort_order' as never)
      .execute();
    return rows as unknown as Omit<SysReportRecord, 'sql_text'>[];
  }

  /** One report by its declared name, or 404. */
  async findByName(name: string): Promise<SysReportRecord> {
    const row = await this.db.kysely
      .selectFrom('sys_report' as never)
      .selectAll()
      .where('name' as never, '=', name as never)
      .executeTakeFirst();
    if (!row) throw new NotFoundException(`No report named "${name}".`);
    return row as unknown as SysReportRecord;
  }

  /**
   * Run a report and return its rows.
   *
   * The query is wrapped rather than concatenated with a LIMIT: a model's query
   * may legitimately end in `ORDER BY`, `LIMIT`, a CTE or a comment, and
   * appending to any of those changes what it means. As a subquery it keeps
   * whatever it already says and the cap applies outside it.
   */
  async run(name: string): Promise<ReportResult> {
    const report = await this.findByName(name);
    assertReadOnly(report.sql_text);

    const started = Date.now();
    // One more than the cap, so a full page can be told from a truncated one
    // without a second count(*) over the same query.
    const capped = `SELECT * FROM (${report.sql_text.replace(/;\s*$/, '')}) AS report_body LIMIT ${MAX_ROWS + 1}`;

    let rows: Record<string, unknown>[];
    try {
      const result = await sql.raw(capped).execute(this.db.kysely);
      rows = (result.rows ?? []) as Record<string, unknown>[];
    } catch (error: any) {
      // The query came from the model, so a failure here is a defect in the
      // document rather than in the request. Say which report and what the
      // database said — an author cannot fix "500 Internal Server Error".
      throw new BadRequestException(
        `Report "${report.name}" failed: ${error?.message ?? String(error)}`,
      );
    }

    const truncated = rows.length > MAX_ROWS;
    if (truncated) rows = rows.slice(0, MAX_ROWS);

    const { sql_text: _sql, ...meta } = report;
    return {
      report: meta,
      // Read off the first row rather than the driver's field list, so the
      // column order a report's SELECT declares is the order it renders in.
      columns: rows.length > 0 ? Object.keys(rows[0]) : [],
      rows,
      rowCount: rows.length,
      truncated,
      durationMs: Date.now() - started,
    };
  }
}

